it is so wonderful to see you today you  know asking the question how are you  such a load of questions these days but  the question is how are you coping you  know I have good days and bad days to be  honest there's just so much heaviness  going on you know the pandemic really is  starting to get to me it seems like it's  getting worse and not better thought  it's a lot it's a lot I'm sure  everybody's looking at us like what is  happening in America I don't know I  don't know either you know it's just  it's all so surreal but you have a way  of using your voice and yesterday I was  sitting there without no distraction and  listened to black like me and I have to  tell you I had tears in my eyes because  I felt the pain and unfortunately so  many people can relate to this song and  he wrote this at a time when armed  robbery things happened to him what does  he represent to you well he represents  my brother my dad my nephew and I lived  in majority white neighborhoods growing  up and that could have been my family  jogging through that neighborhood I had  that song my mom called me and she was  like we need a message of hope right now  I was like mom I played you that song a  year ago I have this song and I just I  didn't know how to go about releasing  something like that because with my  label you know being an artist if you  think it would be a release a song and  it's not there's like a thousand people  you have to go through so before we  released it it came out so suddenly like  the Friday before blackout Tuesday I  called my manager and I was like Eric we  really need to talk about at least start  the discussion of releasing black like  me especially in this time come Monday  morning I wake up I've got a so many  phone calls and they're like we're gonna  release black like me tomorrow and I was  like there you have it that's huge that  the fact that they were willing to do  that like that says a lot of  change as well because in your lyrics  you say I'm all grown up and nothing has  changed and just what you're saying now  there is these little steps that  recovery are making change happen how do  you define change  well I define change by seeing people  actually listening there's so many  conversations over the last month or so  seeing so many black people and people  of color getting a chance to be heard  and a lot of times I feel that people  thought it was just a bunch of noise and  now people are actually listening and  that to me has changed  that's a Hulett people are finally  getting a chance to be heard and to talk  about these issues that have been going  on since way before we were yeah to even  really comprehend them and I love the  way you are so open on your platform you  have so many followers you have so many  fans dedicated fans and you're using  that voice or educating them how was the  reaction when you start talking about  black lives matter movement well you  know of course you get your occasional  parens and Kevin's that say I have  something to say but the overall  response has been absolutely beautiful  when I released black like me my room is  right over there and I stayed in my room  literally the entire day because I was  terrified about the reaction which is  crazy to think that I'm doing about  releasing a song that's my truth there's  nothing more why why were you so  terrified well I think my label as well  as my self was scared of the push back  that I would miss you the hate that I  would receive and I'd still get it but  it's not nearly as bad as I thought it  was at one point the label had even  discussed about possibly getting  security for me and were too angry about  it and that terrified me I was like what  you want security for me like I'm just  I'm just this black girl from Texas why  do I need security you know and that  really really terrified me so I was  expecting this pushback and even when I  wrote the song a year ago I remember one  of the producers Nathan Chapman said to  me I think we just wrote one of the most  important songs of your career and it  might make it really angry so it was a  constant thought that this could  possibly be that and it wasn't it was  actually the exact opposite which is  great for me that has changed yeah you  talked about how everything is so heavy  right now and there's so many trigger  points when you're on social media if  you're watching the news as he said  Brianna Taylor he's no justice for her  at this point you talked about George  Floyd how do you mentally take care of  yourself knowing that you have to  continue this this conversation you know  I have to put social media down I have  to do a lot of just spinning I have four  animals that I spend a lot of time with  writing helps a lot I've really dived  into writing a lot I've got a whole set  up here in LA that I can remotely write  and and get this stuff off of my chest  music really does feel so much and just  spending time with my husband like it is  so heavy you know so I have to  completely take the time to just step  away sometimes and take care of myself  yeah you really do have to step away and  I'll have to give you kudos because  you're a country artist in an industry  where it's hard enough for a woman to  succeed we talked about the radio play  where there's not enough for women to be  on radio and here you are bringing out a  song like this and you talked about  being terrified about it but do you feel  now free almost to be able to speak your  truth in country music I'd ever since I  finally got over the fear of the  pushback for me it's the reason why a  lot of country artists don't really  speak on a lot of matters is how to fear  that they're gonna be canceled out of  this format and that's the way they make  their money that's the way they take  care of their families and for me it was  like I've already hit rock bottom I  can't go any lower I'm just gonna speak  my truth and it doesn't matter what  anybody says because I'm speaking in  I'm not angry about it I'm just speaking  the truth like look it's hard love color  look it's hard for white women and  something needs to change and I  literally will openly say that white  women are being discriminated in country  music just as much as any black person  and sometimes even more and it's  important that we have these  conversations we have to become  uncomfortable to incite change we have  to it's the only way and that's why I've  become free because I want to see change  I really really want to see change  whether anything happens for my career  on I want to see change talk about white  women it brings them it makes me think  about lately Lady Antebellum changing  their names to Lady a the Dixie Chicks  changing it to the chicks and putting a  protest song out there is that consider  the change you need in country music yes  that's that is that's a start I still  wanted more women have major careers I  want to see more women be able to have  Stadium tours and arena tours where they  can take other women out with them that  is the change that I want to see that  I'm starting to see I'm seeing more  women start to get number ones on radio  back to back but that's that's the  change that I want to see seeing more  people like Jimmie Allen get success  seeing's people like myself I would love  to see more women of color whether they  are black whether they are Mexican  whether they are Asian whatever I would  love to see that that is the change that  I would like to see has anyone from the  country music world reached out to you  after releasing the song yes actually a  lot of people have Kaylee hammock has  been one that's been supportive even  Dave from Lady Antebellum reached out to  me to support me there's been a lot of  support in that sense Margo price yeah  Tennille it's nice yeah mr. Newlin what  was it like growing up in Texas well it  was a lot of church a lot of time in  church when I was vacation bible school  whether it was Sunday school whether it  was choir rehearsal  Church that was a lot of church in Maya  Texas Mike and I lived in the country I  lived in off of gravel roads like my  grandmother she lived so deep in the  country that the streets didn't have  names they were numbered  and that was my life my grandmother was  a huge Dolly Parton fan so next my roots  VHS tapes on the back of the door there  were Kenny Rogers and Dolly Parton VHS  tape even in southern movies that was my  life I loved you know being an artist in  in an industry like this where a lot of  girls are looking up to you what is your  advice going forward now in 2020 with  these changes with less fear we still  have the fear less fear going forward my  advice to women that are pursuing a  career in country music or any music  career whatever your ideas are great  ideas do not let all of these people get  in your head and mess with your artistry  because that happened to me and as women  when we grow up we're taught to respect  our elders and majority of the people in  authority are male and there are elders  and a lot of times we tend to cater to  what they want versus what we want and  we must realize that you are the talent  you are the artist  you are the future so stand up for what  you believe stand up for your music  stand up for your artistry and don't  stop don't stop standing up for yourself  and speaking your mind you are so  beautiful and your voice is so beautiful  and thank you for everything you're  doing and absolutely you are changed so  thank you thank you for what you're  doing  you
 

